Behavioral Design Patterns
Order Management System — Spring Boot Implementation
Assignment 2: Chain of Responsibility • Command • Observer • Strategy
 
Behavioral Design Patterns — Order Management System

Assignment 2 implementation, built directly on top of the starter template
from DianaScurtu/design_patterns.
All TODOs from the template are filled in, and the project is refactored
into a Spring Boot layered architecture with H2 persistence, per the
assignment's exercise instructions.

Patterns implemented

Pattern	Where	Role in this system
Chain of Responsibility	handler/	OrderValidationHandler (abstract) → InventoryCheckHandler → PaymentValidationHandler. Each order runs through this chain before it can be placed.
Command	command/	OrderCommand / PlaceOrderCommand encapsulates "place this order" as an object, executed by OrderService.
Observer	notification/	NotificationService (subject) notifies every registered Observer (EmailNotification, SmsNotification) when an order is placed or its status changes.
Strategy	payment/	PaymentStrategy / CreditCardPayment / PayPalPayment, selected at runtime via PaymentStrategyFactory based on the order's paymentMethod.


OrderService (service/OrderService.java) is where all four meet: it
runs the validation chain, resolves and invokes a payment strategy,
executes the place-order command, and notifies observers — all without
depending on any single concrete handler, strategy, or observer.

Layered architecture

controller/   OrderController        — @RestController, REST endpoints
service/      OrderService           — @Service, business logic + pattern orchestration
repository/   OrderRepository        — Spring Data JPA, backed by H2
entity/       Order                  — @Entity
dto/          OrderDto, StatusUpdateRequest
utils/        OrderMapper            — entity <-> DTO conversion
handler/      Chain of Responsibility classes
command/      Command pattern classes
notification/ Observer pattern classes
payment/      Strategy pattern classes
exception/    OrderValidationException, GlobalExceptionHandler (@RestControllerAdvice)


REST API

Method	Path	Description
POST	/api/orders	Place a new order (runs validation → payment → command → notification)
GET	/api/orders	List all orders
GET	/api/orders/{id}	Get one order
PUT	/api/orders/{id}/status	Update an order's status (e.g. SHIPPED, CANCELLED)
DELETE	/api/orders/{id}	Delete an order


Example request body for POST /api/orders:

{
  "customerName": "Ada Lovelace",
  "totalAmount": 249.99,
  "paymentMethod": "CREDIT_CARD"
}


A rejected order (e.g. "paymentMethod": "BITCOIN") returns
400 Bad Request with a JSON error body, via GlobalExceptionHandler.

Swagger UI is available at /swagger once the app is running; the H2
console is at /h2-console (JDBC URL jdbc:h2:mem:orderdb, user sa,
password password, per application.properties).

Logging

All System.out.println calls from the original template are replaced
with @Slf4j-backed logging (log.info(...), log.warn(...)), per the
assignment's logging instruction.

